  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1246 PM MDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nSunday...Current Heat Advisory looks on track. Primary challenge \nwill be the heat, under the dominant ridge aloft, with record high \ntemperatures likely at Laramie, Cheyenne, Chadron, Alliance, \nScottsbluff and Sidney, as 700 mb temperatures from 20 to 23 Celsius \nyield maximum temperatures in the lower 90s west of I-25, with 97 to \n106 degrees east of I-25.\n\nRecord high temperatures for July 26th include the following current \nrecords...\n\n100 degrees in 2025 at Alliance...105 degrees in 2021 at Chadron... \n104 degrees in 1935 at Scottsbluff...99 degrees in 2011 at Sidney... \n89 degrees in 2016 at Laramie...and 95 degrees in 1910 at Cheyenne.\n\nMonday...Strong ridging aloft continues with near record high \ntemperatures at Cheyenne, Chadron, Alliance and Sidney using 700 mb \ntemperatures from 19 to 22 Celsius. Thus current Heat Advisory looks \non track.\n\nRecord high temperatures for July 27th include the following current \nrecords...\n\n104 degrees in 1931 at Alliance...106 degrees in 1931 at Chadron... \n102 degrees in 1975 at Scottsbluff...101 degrees in 2012 at Sidney \nand 94 degrees in 1960 at Cheyenne. \n\nTuesday...Cooler temperatures expected as low and mid level moisture \nincreases along with cloud cover. Monsoonal type moisture and \ndiurnal and differential orographic heating will produce scattered \nto numerous showers and scattered thunderstorms, most numerous \nacross and near the Snowy, Sierra Madre and Laramie Ranges.\n\nWednesday...A passing shortwave trough aloft will combine with \nplenty of low and mid level atmospheric moisture to produce \nscattered afternoon and evening showers and beneficial rains, \nprimarily over and near the mountains.\n\nThursday...Ridging aloft reasserts itself over Wyoming and Colorado, \nwith drying in the atmosphere noted on the models, thus will see a \ndecrease in late day shower and thunderstorm coverage, with areal \ncoverage highest over and near the mountains.\n\nFriday...The flow aloft turns northerly, with the bulk of the \nmonsoonal moisture moving into western Nebraska, and drier air \ninfiltrating southeast Wyoming, thus it seems likely that showers \nand thunderstorms will eventually be confined to western Nebraska, \nwith lesser chances over southeast Wyoming.",
